From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Fri, 21 Jan 94 22:22:14 WET
Subject: [94-1-393] Self Awareness is Bunk

Peter C. McCluskey writes:
> 
> Ray writes:
>  >    Most scientists spend years to decades to come up with a theory.
>  > These theories just don't pop into people's heads. The seed of such
>  > ideas do but my bet is, if you are prepared at the moment of inspiration
>  > to record your thoughts, you'd be able to describe how you thought of it.
>  
>  Jacques Hadamard's book "The Psychology of Invention in the Mathematical
> Field", has a number of reports from famous mathematicians and scientists
> which suggest the exact opposite of your claim. Particularly memorable is
> the description of Poincare's development of an important theorem. He says
> the full solution to a problem that had been bothering him for a while
> suddenly emerged into his consciousness while he was boarding a bus and
> thinking about something unrelated.

I said the "seeds of the idea do" but the complete theory doesn't. That
takes years of work. "bothering him for a while" suggests he worked on it for 
a long time. I too have worked one problems for a long time and had a key 
solution occur to me while I was concentrating on something else. This is 
quite a bit different than a grand unified theory completely worked out 
appearing in your head. I offer Kepler as a counter claim to Poincare.

  My own theory of the Poincare situation is that his mind already had
the solution figured out subsonciously, but that the concious mind
took a while to parse it into an understandable form. I still say
one can figure out the connections of where the idea came from if one is
prepared to explore that at the moment of inspiration. Most new ideas
are merely permutations and combinations of existing ideas with a random
element. The random element may be unexplainable, but somewhere you
have made an association with existing ideas and that is the path you can
follow. (Consider creativity as a genetic program. Tracking down the
creation of a new idea only requires looking at previous ideas)

  Anyway, even if I am wrong, this still cannot lead one to the conclusion
that self-awareness is bunk, dangerous, or that Jupiter brains can't
have more of it without going insane.




