Thank you, Dan, for this realization (you must have been deleting that
contentious series of posts I had with KW, as I would have had I not
been involved). It now appears that we are on the same wavelength here.

> I tend to think of intimate experiences as joint property, and so
> avoid sharing them freely with anyone who may have later contact with
> the person I shared the experience with. Of course, it is a mistake to
> assume that others will share my sense of propriety, so if revelations
> would bug me, I should make sure up front that the person acts as I do
> or avoid them in the first place. I can of course complain after the
> fact that they had done me wrong, but why bother unless I have an
> interest in continuing the relationship?

Yes, exactly. With many of the people I meet, including many on this
list, I find myself having no interest in hearing about their daily
lives, their thoughts, etc. This obviously doesn't mean they're wrong
or clueless, and that I'm right and clued-in, it just means what it
means. I thus try to avoid these people, speak noncomittally to them,
etc.

Some folks get very upset at this. I suspect this has a lot to do with
Jung's characterization of "introverts" and "extroverts," which we've
discussed on this list before. As an introvert (despite my vocal
comments and my volume of posts), I judge ideas and people by my
internal standards and my internal level of interest. Extroverts, even
if shy (I'm deliberately noting that introvert and extrovert do _not_
mean what many people think they mean), often want others to like them
and are anxious/eager to communicate with others. We introverts just
try to steer clear of these extroverts.

An electronic forum like this list, and many other cyberspatial
arenas, has the problem that people can't as easily avoid each other.
Kill files are a partial solution. But where in a fact to face
encounter we can wander away from disagreeable conversations, and
just shrug, here on this list there is almost always someone who will
disagree with anything one says. 

And when people being "sharing" their reactions to others, including
those who just want to avoid them (for whatever reason), the problems
get much worse. That's why I think overly personal comments are
uncalled-for.

By the way, I support Jamie Dinkelacker's excellent example of a
private party, where one takes a person aside and asks them to cool it
with their "sharings," and I support as well Steve Whitrow's equally
clear analysis tonight of the subte but important issues of illegal
vs. immoral vs. inappropriate behavior. I agree that these are often
confused, and I think libertarians and extropians need to think more
about these isssues.

Libertarians focus on legal vs. illegal, freedom vs. state rule
issues, and ignore issues of propriety, politeness, etc.  (As well
they should, in the sense that these are issues the body politic
should stay out of.) But something is being lost if we say it is "OK"
for overly personal "sharings" and psychodrama revelations to appear
on the list.

(I'm not targeting any particular person here, no matter how it may
appear. My recent disputes with KW only illustrate a more basic issue
I have, and apparently others do as well, with the whole "say
whatever's on your mind" and "play your tapes" (a California
psychobabble term I'll explain to anyone who asks) mentality. Some
things are better left unsaid.)

--Tim May, diagnosed introvert


