From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Fri, 21 Jan 1994 20:46:43 -0800
Subject: [94-1-396] Branching grammars

vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner) writes:

>Does anyone know of a simple, compact, representation for a grammar
>whose sentences can contain branches (converging and diverging)?
>
>The only thing I can think of is to draw pictures with symbols and
>arrows, but that isn't desirable for a number of reasons.
>
>Any ideas?

How about sentences or phrases with arrows?

I do what I think you're asking when I write articles or sales copy. 
Basically I leave lots of blank space between the lines of my first draft
and then write alternate phrases starting from branches of the 
a1 --> {a2, b2, c2,...} variety. Then I draw arrows to the next sentence or
phrase in the thread, sometimes creating merges of the form 
{a1, b1, c1,...} --> a2. Then I read it out loud along the various threads
until I like the result.

This is trivially easy to display with pencil and paper but harder to spell
out here in ASCII text. A more streamlined approach for the reader would be
to simply list all the phrases seperately then draw arrows but I don't
think as clearly that way.



