Date: Fri, 21 Jan 1994 23:54:05 -0800
Subject: [94-1-399] Branching grammars

>You might consider graphs grammars.  They may be too restrictive, but
>may lead you in a useful direction.

I don't know what these are.  Could you be more specific, or post an
example of a sentence in such a grammar?  (A good test for an
appropriate grammar is that it's sentences would be easily postable
through a "text-only" bottleneck such as this one.)

>How about sentences or phrases with arrows?
> [etc]
>Basically I leave lots of blank space between the lines of my first
>draft and then write alternate phrases starting from branches of the
>a1 --> {a2, b2, c2,...} variety. Then I draw arrows to the next
>sentence or phrase in the thread, sometimes creating merges of the
>form {a1, b1, c1,...} --> a2.
> [etc]
>A more streamlined approach for the reader would be to simply list all
>the phrases seperately then draw arrows

This sounds more like pictures, or at least something 2-d.  However, I
think you may be getting to a fundamental point, in that it may not be
possible to do this with only one string, and it may require listing
several strings.  Actually, what you describe sounds like a program.
This would be acceptable as a linear text grammar representation, as
long as it's short.

Another represnetation potentially "good" for small sentences might be
a recursive one which embeds alternatives within the string instead of
referencing them from additional symbols or multiple statements.
However, this representation requires more parsing to decode and is
harder to read.

For example, the sentence:

  A     C     E
   \   / \   /
    \ /   \ /
     B-----D-----F
    /     /
   /     /            (note that this was hard to do in emacs,
  G     H              and may not even look right on your machine)

might be repesented by the string:

({A|G}B{C})|H)D{E|F}         (assuming an implicit "join" 
                              after sets of alternatives)

but that's not pretty, and may become very cumbersome with bigger
sentences. 

