From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sat, 22 Jan 94 09:31:29 GMT
Subject: [94-1-404] Self Awareness is Bunk

John k Clark, talking about self-awareness:

> The fact that nature has not done something is far
> from a rigorous proof that it's impossible in principle but it
> is enough to make me suspicious.

By that logic you should be suspicious of the atom bomb, jet flight,
skyscrapers....  Nature has explored such a tiny fraction of what is
possible that this a dangerous principle to use under any circumstances.

The converse that if nature has done something then so can we is, of
course, perfectly acceptable and, for me, an expression of dynamic
optimism (Sorry Tim!).  

Your use of nature as a guide is especially inappropriate for a property
that nature _has_ created (limited self-awareness) which we are seeking
to merely amplify.  The most natural explanation of self-awareness says
that it is a function of the complexity of the internal representations
of a creature's environment.  The more intelligent or complex an
organism's internal mental representations are the more subtle will be
its models of itself - unless we have deliberately placed a block
against such self-models.

By this logic a Jupiter-sized brain will naturally be vastly more 
self-aware than we are.

It is also logically false to infer from evolution that because no more
"advanced" organisms than ourselves have evolved that there may be some
barrier to their existence.  For more advanced beings to exist lesser
beings must have existed as ancestors.  Those ancestors are ourselves!

