From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sat, 22 Jan 94 15:30:37 GMT
Subject: [94-1-405] African predators

Having refreshed my memory of the habits of man-eating tigers and
leopards by re-reading Jim Corbett's accounts of hunting man-eaters I
wish to correct something I said earlier.  I said:

> The cubs of man-eaters often become man-eaters in turn and
> particularly dangerous.

This is not completely true.  Cubs of man-eaters do become man-eaters
in turn - but only whilst living with mom.  This may last well into
adulthood (especially if mom has difficulty hunting for herself) but
once left to fend for themselves (after mom's demise) they invariably
revert to hunting non-humans.

This is the observation of Jim Corbett who, as I mentioned earlier,
spent over 40 years tracking and hunting man-eaters in India.  He also
had some interesting observations on the different habits of leopard and
tiger man-hunters.

When a tiger turns man-eater, for whatever reason, it permanently loses
its timidity of humans.  Consequently it is not afraid to face a human
and hunt them by day.  Attacks on people are made in broad daylight when
it is easiest for the tiger to hunt.  The tiger is therefore on the move
by day and this is best time to stalk it at well.  A man-eating leopard,
by contrast, never loses its fear of man and when forced to turn man-
eater makes its kills by night.  It is afraid to face humans by
daylight, often killing sleeping humans, be they in houses, huts or open
air under cover of darkness.  Man-eating leopards, therefore, roam most
at night and are much harder to follow and track than tigers.

It seems, from the above, that leopards have a more deeply ingrained
instinctive fear of humans, whereas the tiger's tendency to shun humans
is more a culturally transmitted or weaker phobia.  This may relate to
the idea that animals that co-evolved in Africa with early hominids may
have evolved an instinctive fear of man, as a defense strategy.  True
leopards (ignoring cheetahs and panthers for the moment) are found,
today, widely in Africa and across Asia.  The cheetah (sometimes called
the Hunting Leopard and very closely related to the true leopard) is
only present in Africa.  This suggests that the leopard and cheetah
evolved in Africa with the leopard recently spreading out into Asia as
well.  Tigers, by contrast, are believed to have diversified from
ancestral feline stock in Northern Eurasia.  Then they moved south,
spreading into China and India, but never, so far as I know, into Africa
- so they have not had so long to develop the same instinctive dread of
humans.

