Geoff Dale wrote: <The reason I had to resort to the Principles and early issues
of Extropy was your desire to ascribe absolute morals to extropianism. In
addition, you seemed to be whipping yourself into a zealotry on the outreach
issue.>

You did not understand what I was saying.  Let me repeat briefly.  There is no
good or bad, no morals at all, no value, no purpose, no meaning in objective
reality.  The objective universe is just unweighted facts.  In consensual
reality, which mediates between objective reality and the subjective reality of
our internal self, meaning arises through communication of private values and
purposes.  This is where concepts come from whether mathematics, logic,
rationality or morality.  The concept of "good" is a moral absolute.  It is not
possible to eliminate the concept of "good" in consensual reality.  This has
nothing to do with extropianism as a movement, it has to do with truth.

As to the charge of zealotry on the outreach issue, I plead guilty unashamedly.
I want an acceleration of the rate at which people world-wide are changing from
entropic death-worshipping to extropic life-preserving.  The faster this change
occurs, the faster the technological achievement of the boundless expansion of
the ego will occur.  I see outreach as literally throwing out life-preservers to
those afflicted by purposelessness and meaninglessness (the "French disease" and
other entropic meme sets).

Geoff: <Many others of you may have been "ur-extropians", or have had the
philosophy without knowing the label, even before the group was formed.>

This association of the "philosophy" with the "label" at a distinct point in
time (the "creation" of the group), is a warning sign to me of religious
tendencies in the form of mythologizing a beginning, a revelation, artificial
instant tradition-making.  These mechanisms can easily lead to dogma down the
road.  The philosophy is in the making as Max is at pains to point out.  The
label is just a label.  In fact, "extropy" started out as "negentropy" coined by
the physicist Erwin Schrodinger, "syntropy" coined by the biologist Albert
Szent-Gyorgyi, and was popularized by Buckminster Fuller and Arthur Koestler in
the 60s and 70s.  "Extropy" is closely analogous to the concept "elan vital" by
the philosopher Henri Bergson early this century and even "entelechy" by the
philosopher Aristotle and the German biologist Hans Driesch.  The philosophy, as
it is forming, is simply a cobbling together of miscellaneous conceptual odds
and ends that have been kicking around for years waiting for some people to
create coherency out of them.  Spontaneous ordering of coherent philosophies
only comes about through much sustained energy input from many different
subjective perspectives reaching towards objective reality (aka "pan-critical
rationalism" within consensual reality).  Very much work remains to be done to
ensure that elements within the philosophy are not working at cross-purposes.  I
accept your explanation for why you quoted chapter and verse to me, I just want
you to know that you are preaching to the choir.

<Just don't tell me that I have a certain set of morals because you say I must.
That is where I objected to your arguments.>

You objected to a lot more than this.  I will repeat that the Principles call
for toleration and self-restraint.  These are moral values.  I desire to see
Nick Szabo's additional moral values of courtesy and empathy added.  Extropic
social virtues, if you will.

<Thank you for the ad hominem.>

You are welcome.  If you and Romana are tired of calling me a "perpetually
confused, crazed charlatan," I will desist from calling you "fools" or
"sycophants."  Call it my 'Tit-for-Tat' strategy.

<I simply refuse to continue communicating with you on this list.>

I am glad you changed your mind.

